AB  - A 4 year old female Irish Wolfhound was splenectomised as a result of an isolated splenic torsion with haemorrhagic infarction. Two weeks following this surgery, the dog developed a pleural effusion and atelectatic areas in the lung. the patient was discharged with a diagnosis of idiopathic pleural effusion, following a favourable response to conservative therapy. Approximately 5 months later the dog experienced a clinical relapse, presenting with a reduced general condition, hyperuresis, tachycardia and pale mucous membrane. As found in the previous examinations, a severe pleural effusion and alveolar lung markings, with air bronchograms, were detected. A thoracotomy was performed as a result of a tentative diagnosis of a lung lobe torsion.
   A 360 degrees torsion of the right cranial lobe of the lung was confirmed. The lobe was resected and the patient died five days after surgery, for unknown reasons.
