AB  - Background: The authors present the clinical manifestations of the torsion of the spleen and the liver lobe in dogs through their own cases. The splenic torsion was diagnosed in young (5 years old) German shepheid male, the hepatic lobe torsion was seen in a female mastine napoletano (1.5 years old).
   Objectives: These acute diseases showed the signs of shock by the physical examination (hyperthermia, anaemia, increased capilary refiling time, abdominal pain in th epigastric region). General weakness and heamaturia were also diagnose. The laboratory tests indicated anaemia, inflammation (increased level of the white blood cells) and elevated level of the liver enzymes by the blood analysis. The exact diagnosis was confirmaed by abdominai ultrasonography in both cases.
   Results and Discussion: In the case of torsion the ultrasonographic structure of the spleen and the liver lobe are typicat decreased levels of echogencity and dilated, thrombotic blood vessels with thickend and echogenic walls in the parenchyma pemitonitis (increased echogenicity of the pentoneum) and free abdominal flud are also prsented in such. Abscess in the liver parenchyma is very rare in dogs but it was also diagnosed in this case. After the operation the prognosis is good in the cases of splenic or hepatic lobe torsion, but became poor in chronic cases, in the case of reperiusion or septic complications.
