AB  - Background: Wandering spleen is a rare clinical entity characterized by an anomalous position of the spleen in the abdomen due to impaired splenic ligamentous attachments that produce an elongation of the vascular pedicle, favoring a possible volvulus of partial or complete vascular axis.
   Clinical presentation is variable, from an asymptomatic patient to another patient with chronic abdominal pain or acute abdomen. The most common complication is acute torsion of the splenic pedicle causing acute abdominal pain. Clinical case: We present the case of a 30-year-old female who arrived at the Emergency Department with acute abdominal pain. Diagnostic imaging including ultrasonography and computed tomography revealed a huge spleen in an abnormal location and with absence of vascularization. Urgent splenectomy was performed.
   Conclusion: Acute torsion of wandering spleen is a rare cause of acute abdomen, making early diagnosis difficult. This can result in delayed treatment and would involve performing a splenectomy due to the presence of splenic necrosis or infarction, increasing the risk of postsplenectomy sepsis.
