AB  - The successful treatment of a primary spleen-torsion in the case of a 3-year-old female Great Dane is presented. The diagnosis - primary torsion of the spleen - was made solely on the basis of the physical examination that revealed a swollen and painful cranial abdomen and an increase in abdominal circumference. An emergency laporotomy was performed, without prior sonographic or radiologic examination. It was not possible to investigate the splenic circulation or to determine the laboratory parameters of ascitic fluid due to the extremely poor general condition of the patient which made immediate surgery necessary. The intensely swollen spleen, with a torque of 360 degrees was removed, together with the greater omentum. The post-operative course was uneventful. Fluid therapy was initated and the patient was monitored closely. The patient was returned to the owner on day 3 post op. Ten months the dog had fully recoverd, so that its duties as a guard-dog are not impaired. Pathological and histological examination of the spleen showed evidence of blood-clotting with haemorrhagic infarction in the oedematous splenic pulp. This must have taken place within the 5 days period of illness prior to surgery. Additional foci of residual fibrosis due to blood-extravasation. suggest that circulatory and/or congestional problems may have been present before the torsion occurred. The question as to whether these were possible causal factors, directly or indirectly for the spleen-torsion, is considered.
